{
  "heading": "Helpers, utilities and private methods",
  "body": "GET /checkout returns {\"total\":42}. When two classes share code, or one class has a long job, ask one question: does this code need other classes (stores, clients, settings or other helpers), saved state, OnStart or OnStop, or a Fake in tests? If the answer is yes, write a helper. A helper is a class that other classes use for part of their work. Mark it with Helper, put it in client/helpers or server/helpers, and end its name with Helper. Tachyon creates it and gives it to each class that asks for it with Needs. A view or a controller does not call a helper directly: it calls a service, and the service calls the helper.\n\nTwo kinds of helper are owned by the class that creates them. In Tac, a helper works in JavaScript, TypeScript, Dart, Rust, Python, Swift, C# and F#. A Swift helper is created by the `@Needs` field that names it, so each class that needs it owns its own instance; it needs an `init()` without arguments and has no OnStart, OnStop or OnEvent. A C# or F# Tac helper is created by its caller with `new`, and the caller owns it: these two languages refuse an injected helper, a helper lifetime and helper hooks (ADR 0091). Go, Kotlin, Java, Ruby and Lua Tac code has no helper yet (TY1308, and TY1405 in Java). In Yon, a helper that its caller builds with `new` stays owned by that caller: Tachyon does not inject it, so its OnStart, OnStop and OnEvent methods never run. To get those hooks, let Tachyon create the helper through Needs or NeedsAll.\n\nIf the answer is no, you do not need a helper:\n\n| The code | Write it as |\n| --- | --- |\n| needs other classes, saved state, OnStart or OnStop, or a Fake in tests | a helper: Helper, in helpers, with a name that ends with Helper |\n| has only static methods and no state | a utility: a class named like PriceUtils in client/shared/utils or server/utils, with no annotation (see Utilities) |\n| is used by one class only | a private method of that class |\n| is called by a view or a controller | a service |\n\nThe layers go in one order: a view or a controller calls a service, a service calls a helper, and a helper calls a store or a client. Any layer can call a utility. The build refuses a dependency that points up, such as a helper that needs a service (TY1327, see Dependencies point down).\n\nIn this example, CheckoutService adds the tax through TaxHelper. TaxHelper is a helper because it needs another class, TaxRateHelper. Only CheckoutService uses the subtotal, so the subtotal is a private method of CheckoutService, not a helper and not a utility. The controller calls the service, never the helper.\n\nHelper was called Component before. The name changed because client/components holds views. The build refuses Component with its new name (TY1308) and a file in a delegates folder with the helpers folder (TY1310). `ty migrate fix --write` moves the files from delegates to helpers, renames each class from a name that ends with Component to a name that ends with Helper, and changes the Needs fields and types that use it.",
